> I have two problems / questions, firstly why is the compiler choosing
> to inline the function given the error message that it reports?

    Because the inlining is chosen before the uninitialized condition
    is detected.  There is no way to "unmake" the decision.  Also,
    the diagnostic is not an error, just a warning letting the user know
    that some uninitialized variable has been fetched.  In this case
    it seems a compiler bug is causing the storage to become
    uninitialized.

> Secondly how can I restructure the code to prevent this error?

    Probably the easiest way is to add the -inline none switch.  This
    will prevent inlinning which seems to be the cause of the problem.

    This compiler bug sounds somewhat familiar.  I believe we have
    corrected it, but I'm not certain if the fix has been released yet.
    As your example did not provide a complete reproducer, I can not
    be sure.  I would suggest you download the latest C compiler.  A
    link can be found at the DTK web site: http://h30097.www3.hp.com/dtk/

    If this compiler does not correct the problem, it should be reported via
    formal support channels.

