  -b batchfile
       Batch mode reads a series of commands from an input batchfile instead
       of stdin.

Looks like leaving the "-b /dev/stdin" out completely would work. The
problem is that sftp may not be written intelligently and to only act
as if it's interactive if input is from a terminal. Given that this
option even exists, when you should be able to just run

	sftp [user@]host < batchfile

I suspect that's the case, but you can try.

> exist, and I'm not sure what to substitute on a Tru64 box in order to
> make this work.   I have tried:
> 
>  sftp -b `tty` somehost <<HEREDOC
> 
> but to no avail.  

No, that wouldn't work. `tty` (or /dev/tty, which should act the same as
the /dev/... string that comes out of `tty`) is always the terminal, and
never anything you can redirect... that's what it's for, after all.

You can create an equivalent to "/dev/stdin" using a named pipe, but
really the best way around this is something like:

	BATCHFILE=/some/secure/directory/batch$$.sftp
	trap 'rm $BATCHFILE' 0		# to guarantee it's cleaned up

	cat > $BATCHFILE << EOF
	commands
	EOF
	sftp -b $BATCHFILE user@host

> lrwx------    1 cj037    cj037          64 Dec 22 13:44 0 ->
> /dev/pts/2

/proc/self/0 is not going to always be linked to the tty. In fact, if that's
linked to the tty in *any* case where stdin is redirected I would be utterly
shocked. Try "ls -l /proc/self/0 < /some/file" and see what happens.

> Does anyone have any idea how to accomplish this under Tru64?  Thanks!

See above. You have a non-portable example. The way to make it portable is
to fix sftp to do what most portable programs do... use the file named "-"
as a special case for stdin... or to use an explicit batch file.

