Bruno Agneray <bruno.agneray@hp.com> typed:
> Hello,
>
> A customer using C2 security, set the u_minchg to 7 days (minimum
> change time interval before a user can change is password again)
> in /etc/auth/system/default.
>
> Regularly, users change their password, and regularly, few days
> after (less than u_minchg ;-)) they have forgotten it.
>
> So they call the administrator to 'reset' a new password.
>
> Unfortunatly, when the administrator force change of the users
> password, it's failed because of the u_minchg.
>
> Is it normal ?
>
> Is there a way to authorize root to change the users password
> ignoring u_minchg ?
>
> Thanks,
>
> Bruno

Root can run edauth, so all restrictions are bypassable. If the users
are really forgetting the password often enough for it to be a problem
then write yourself a little script which resets the u_succhg setting
to 'today - 7 days' and then runs passwd.
If you want to script the entire thing then perl knows about time_t
values, as does gnu awk and gnu date.
HTH
Adam




